{"name":"Nickel Ore Green","hex":"#537e7e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nickel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A silvery elemental metal with an atomic number of 28 and symbol Ni.","A coin worth 5 cents."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Synonym of cheap: Low price and/or low value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nickel"},{"term":"ore","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Orejón."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Rock or other material that contains valuable or utilitarian materials; primarily a rock containing metals or gems for which it is typically mined and processed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ore"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nickel","blocks":[{"text":"Nickel is a chemical element; it has symbol Ni and atomic number 28. It is a silvery-white lustrous metal with a slight golden tinge. Nickel is a hard and ductile transition metal. Pure nickel is chemically reactive, but large pieces are slow to react with air under standard conditions because a passivation layer of nickel oxide that prevents further corrosion forms on the surface. Even so, pure native nickel is found in Earth's crust only in tiny amounts, usually in ultramafic rocks, and in the interiors of larger nickel–iron meteorites that were not exposed to oxygen when outside Earth's atmosphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Nickel_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Nickel_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nickel","wikidata":"Q744"},{"title":"Ore","blocks":[{"text":"Ore is natural rock or sediment that contains one or more valuable minerals, typically including metals, concentrated above background levels, and that is economically viable to mine and process. Ore grade refers to the concentration of the desired material it contains. The value of the metals or minerals a rock contains must be weighed against the cost of extraction to determine whether it is of sufficiently high grade to be worth mining and is therefore considered an ore. A complex ore is one containing more than one valuable mineral."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Banded_iron_formation.png/330px-Banded_iron_formation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ore","wikidata":"Q102798"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Ni%40CNT2.jpg/330px-Ni%40CNT2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Ni%40CNT2.jpg/330px-Ni%40CNT2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Electron micrograph of a Ni nanocrystal inside a single wall carbon nanotube; scale bar 5 nm[19]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANi%40CNT2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/500px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/1280px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Widmanstatten_hand.jpg/500px-Widmanstatten_hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Widmanstätten pattern showing the two forms of nickel–iron, kamacite and taenite, in an octahedrite meteorite","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWidmanstatten_hand.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Structure_of_hexacyanodinickelate%28I%29_ion.png/500px-Structure_of_hexacyanodinickelate%28I%29_ion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Structure_of_hexacyanodinickelate%28I%29_ion.png/1280px-Structure_of_hexacyanodinickelate%28I%29_ion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Structure_of_hexacyanodinickelate%28I%29_i